> It would be better to
> use git-send-email(1) directly, with a mailer program that delivers
> messages to the maildir.

Agreed. Most of the parts already exist. format-patch.sh still earns
its place, because it adds the Change-Id and the interdiff, and plain
git format-patch can do neither. But its output is ordinary patch
files, and git send-email takes files. The agent step would become

    format-patch.sh -o /tmp/series ... &&
    git send-email --to=... /tmp/series/*.patch

with sendemail.sendmailCmd or sendemail.smtpServer set in git config
instead of on the command line. That replaces the hand-written delivery
loop in prompt.md and retires mail-send.sh.

> It may be that I need a local SMTP server after
> all, because the agent nodes won't have direct access to the
> maildir. Instead they would send to a local SMTP server which delivers
> to maildir, and then mbsync syncs between the local maildir and
> fastmail.

I think a local SMTP server is the right call over mounting the maildir
into agent jails. A write mount would let an agent rename or delete any
message on the list. With SMTP, an agent can only add one.

For the server, I recommend OpenSMTPD (mail/opensmtpd):

- [fact, from smtpd.conf(5)] It delivers to a maildir natively
  ("action ... maildir"). It matches on source and recipient in two
  lines, e.g. accept from the jail network for lab-devel@p7x.dev and
  reject everything else.
- [inference] The lab-mail spec rejected queuing MTAs because a queue
  answers 250 before Fastmail has seen the message, so a rejection
  turns into a bounce nobody sees. That objection goes away here. The
  only hop is the local disk, and a local maildir does not reject
  mail.
- [inference] dma, the FreeBSD base MTA, will not do. It does not listen
  on the network, and as far as I know it delivers local mail only to
  mbox. Postfix can do maildir too, but it is much larger than this
  job needs.

The safety property also gets stronger. Today smtprelay keeps agents
off other addresses with an allowlist in front of a real Fastmail
login. A server that only ever delivers locally cannot send anywhere
external at all. Nothing an agent sends leaves the host, except as a
file that mbsync copies into a Fastmail folder.

Things the proposal needs to settle:

1. Moderation. Today an agent's mail reaches the list only after you
   merge its `submissions` push into mail@upstream, and that merge is
   your gate. If OpenSMTPD delivers straight into the maildir that
   mbsync syncs, the gate is gone and agent mail is on the list right
   away. One way to keep a gate is to have the server deliver into a
   separate "moderation" maildir folder and move accepted messages
   across yourself. This is the unmoderated-delivery question from
   "What's the status of beastie connection with fastmail?" [1], and
   it is still open.

2. The loop signal. lab-process-mail keys on the `claude` bookmark at
   submissions. Under this design the signal would be a delivered
   message, e.g. a file in the maildir with the reply's In-Reply-To, or
   `notmuch count` on it. That is still a side effect the model does
   not author, so CLAUDE.md's "Own the loop" holds, but lab-process-mail
   and prompt.md both have to change.

3. mbsync settings. [inference] To start, I would run it with Expunge
   None and Remove None, so a deletion or an expunge on one side never
   destroys mail on the other. Then turn on what you need once the
   sync has proven itself. notmuch copes with the flag renames mbsync
   makes, as long as `notmuch new` runs after each sync.

4. What becomes of lab-mail. If agents stop sending through Fastmail,
   then smtprelay, the credentials mount, and the mailcred group in
   "[PATCH v11 0/8] jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the mail relay
   jail" [2] are only needed for your own mail to people outside the
   list. The Dovecot mirror in the same series becomes redundant with
   a local mbsync. "[PATCH v2] dotfiles/notmuch: Spec reading and
   sending through lab-mail" assumes the relay, so it is stale under
   either outcome.

Your follow-up says this design is very close to the git-based one,
just without git push and pull, and I agree. The difference that
matters is that mbsync merges message by message, so it never
conflicts. In exchange you lose the review gate and the history that
jj gives today, unless point 1 adds a gate back.

Status: research and feedback only; no patch.
Asks: decide on point 1, whether agent mail is moderated, before
anyone writes a spec. Once you choose, I will write the spec covering
OpenSMTPD, mbsync, and the git send-email change, with an alternatives
section.

